					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2721810&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607570898" alt="Buttfranklin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2721810)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/buttfranklin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2721810); return false;" >Buttfranklin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1471905087 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Aug 2016 22:31</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2571230">
									

<p>The addendum is the strength of this article - it tells a really nice story. However, that Description section could be much improved, right down to the level of grammar. Examples that come to mind first: it should be &quot;Arabic numerals&quot;, with a capital A; that semicolon in the list of behaviours doesn't belong there; you need to think about comma usage generally - for example, I'd personally say &quot;and, depending on [the] species, hunt&quot;.</p>
<p>And there's plenty of scope for zhooshing it up with more precise, technical language. Saying &quot;the top quarter <span style="text-decoration: underline;">has</span> a sun and moon&quot; isn't great. Something like &quot;[figures/forms/illustrations] [corresponding to/analogous to/similar in appearance and behaviour to] Earth's Sun and Moon [can be observed/are depicted/are present]&quot; would be better tonally. Remember: on this site, you can't be too anal!</p>
<p>The addendum is already pretty great, but there's still room for improvement. (Warning: nitpicky, probably plain-wrong section incoming.) I feel that sprinkling in a few more details about the flames and the navy humanoids would make the reader fear them more. It's a fine line, obviously, because you need to leave some mystery&#8230; (Am I missing some reference to another scip?) A few more details throughout might be nice. Also, you might want to experiment with some tried-and-tested storytelling devices to make us identify with the &quot;goodies&quot; <em>even</em> more. e.g. what if you identify one of the figures as having distinctive red hair, and then establish them as a heroic character with some suitably heroic gesture, and then <em>they're</em> the one who's tragically killed in battle with the Smilodon? That sort of thing.</p>
<p>But on the whole: neat idea, nicely implemented - just work on that Description, please!</p>

							</div>

								
